Oh, now there's a tree over here, I've got to turn right to get between them.

00:31:45.060 --> 00:32:01.160
Like, it is a little tough to find, but in the third mission, where you're dodging bolts of lightning and later lasers from the spacecraft, I guess, some of that, I mean, that is, like, a real test of Twitch reflexes where I feel like it's not even, like, for casual gamers anymore.

00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:03.820
Like, you really have to be dead on.

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:10.180
Granted, I did learn that if I just constantly hit left, right, left, right, left, right, I usually luck my way through it.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:18.420
But if I try to legitimately dodge those lightning bolts, I mean, it's possible, but it is very, I don't think I could do it without a lot of practice.

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:28.840
On that note, how often did you start a mission and then within several seconds of taking off, be greeted with a missile in your face and a game over?

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:38.760
That happened to me a lot at first until I realized that my strategy to that is not even to barrel roll, but to, like, horizontally spin.

00:32:39.160 --> 00:32:41.900
Whenever a missile's coming, I would just spin horizontally, it would go by me.

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:49.960
So after a while, I was able to avoid that most of the time, but it was at the expense of me ever being able to fly straight.

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:54.900
Okay. Game over.

00:32:56.140 --> 00:33:00.820
On the sequels and spinoff side, I think last Top Gun episode we covered a bunch of these.

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:10.400
I just wanted to highlight some of the other Nintendo entries that we won't get to, but, or maybe we will if, you know, people demand it enough.

00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:12.700
Top Gun Guts and Glory.

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:15.860
That sounded strange.

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:17.700
Should Guts and Glory.

00:33:17.860 --> 00:33:24.700
I guess it's fine. Game Boy game that attempts to do the whole flight simulation thing that the NES games are doing.

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:27.000
It's a pretty crazy task.

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:37.200
And you'll see from, like, if you just look up Top Gun Guts and Glory, it's almost funny what the aircrafts look like from a distance.

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:44.700
And the fact that they also decide to include, like, now it's just a vertical line that is the horizontal for you.

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:47.320
There's no, there's not even any sense of land.

00:33:47.320 --> 00:33:50.080
It's just the green is the land and the white is the sky.

00:33:50.260 --> 00:34:00.800
But just incredible that they were even able to include something that looked like a cockpit, a score, and try to convey 3D space inside a Game Boy game.

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:06.000
Yeah, it almost looks closer to your radar screen.

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:08.540
Like, it's like if the whole game was the radar screen.

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:12.980
Right, right. I don't have a visual in front of me, but I like what I'm hearing.

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:15.580
The aircraft looks like a little heartbeat.

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:17.940
Oh. You know, like a boom-boom.

00:34:18.400 --> 00:34:19.700
It just looks like a heartbeat.

00:34:19.900 --> 00:34:20.920
Yeah, yeah. Yeah, heartbeat.

00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:22.000
I think you said a harpy.

00:34:22.240 --> 00:34:23.400
No, that'd be cute too, though.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:29.860
Yeah, like a, like it's a, Top Gun is now like an antiquity mythological thing.

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:35.400
But Top Gun wouldn't always just be 3D dogfighting flight simulators.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:45.440
It would eventually move on to Top Gun Firestorm, which is an isometric flight combat game for first the Game Boy Color and then eventually the Game Boy Advance.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:57.180
Not what I was expecting, but also just kind of crazy that Top Gun, a movie from 1987 with no planned sequels at this point, could still be getting video games in 2001.

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:00.460
Like just that the brand was strong enough.

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:04.100
That would be like, I don't know, did they release a Rocky game in 2001?

00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:06.140
They probably did. Well, you know what?

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:10.140
I mean, I guess it's different because there's still this like universe going on.

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:14.580
But even like Lord of the Rings came out in 2001 and they're still making like Gollum games and stuff.

00:35:14.740 --> 00:35:15.840
Yeah, but that's a bigger franchise, right?

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:17.080
I don't think we're putting it on the same.

00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:18.980
We've got Hobbit stuff coming out.

00:35:19.340 --> 00:35:21.320
I mean, I don't think it's unprecedented.

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:34.300
I'm pretty sure that these posthumous, I use posthumous, I guess, semi-jokingly, but franchises aren't really out of nowhere.

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:37.580
Like there was that 2008 like Ghostbusters game or something.

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:38.520
Maybe it was 2008.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:43.940
Right. Before any of these Ghostbuster remakes were planned or shot or anything.

00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:47.320
Like I'm thinking it's not out of the blue.

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:48.240
It's not crazy.

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:55.060
There was a Rocky video game in 2002 and then a follow-up sequel in 2004 called Rocky Legends.

00:35:55.600 --> 00:36:00.700
And hell, there was a Warriors game and there hasn't been a Warriors since the Warriors.

00:36:00.880 --> 00:36:03.260
Yeah, we need Rockstar to go back to making stuff like that.

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:04.960
Make the Warriors again.

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:06.900
You know what we're going to do again?

00:36:07.420 --> 00:36:08.380
Play the Warriors?

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:11.100
Nope. Same thing we do every week, Sean.

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:12.720
The Essential Games List.

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:21.360
I think Top Gun 2 is a reasonable improvement over Top Gun 1 if you just play them back to back.

00:36:22.140 --> 00:36:25.500
I'm surprised at how short the game theoretically is.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:27.500
I think it's, you know, it is a tough game.

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:28.960
I didn't get to the final mission.

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:32.000
It is a tough game to beat or whatever.

00:36:32.100 --> 00:36:36.140
But the actual experience is kind of what you make of it.

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:41.360
It's how much you want to play Top Gun is what you'll get out of it is what I'm trying to say.

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:51.140
And it's not that I didn't have a good time or that I haven't, you know, like, well, what's a better 3D flight simulator game on the NES?

00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:52.780
That's not really the issue at hand.

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:58.420
I think the issue for me with Top Gun 2, the second mission, is that they could have taken a lot more risks than what they did.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:05.480
It does feel weird that we got mostly the first game with some improved graphics and a two-player mode.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:10.440
There are other differences, but I think they could have really expanded that whole adventure mode.

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:12.260
They're the ones calling it adventure mode, not me.

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:19.740
I think they really could have expanded that to feel more like an adventure and tell the next tale in Maverick's Journey than what we got in this game.

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:29.100
So I feel like this would have been, for me, back in that day, if I would have picked up Top Gun 2, it would have been like picking up Super Mario Bros.

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:35.560
2 Japan and being like, oh, it's just the first game, but now you made it crazy hard.

00:37:35.740 --> 00:37:39.620
In this one, it's almost like, oh, it's just the first game, but now you made it a little bit easier.

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:44.540
So, no, it's not on the essential games list for me, but that doesn't mean it's a bad game.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:38:00.800
Sean? Yeah, I agree that this is an improvement over the first one, and I've already gone over how my reaction to this game is colored mostly by the interstitial period between playing Top Gun 1 and Top Gun 2.

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:04.920
I wasn't quite as hot on this game as I was the first one.

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:31.040
But I think what that's taught me is I think that a game like this, at least on the NES, cannot really be essential just because everything it's trying to do has been iterated upon to such an extent that Top Gun 2, sorry, Top Gun 2 mission is just going to kind of blur into every other game that we've played, I think.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:38.340
I'm not going to remember this game, and if I can already tell you that as we're recording the episode, it's not essential.

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:42.580
Joe? I definitely really enjoyed my time with this game.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:49.940
I think, though, when I really consider it, a lot of what I'm enjoying about it is the novelty of it on the NES.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:39:09.180
It is fast and fun in that way, but yeah, I kind of agree with what both of you have said, and I think that if you are playing NES games to see what kind of cool things the NES could do, or just to see something that's impressive on the NES, this is a game to definitely play.

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:15.760
And I think that it's maybe even a play it for anyone, but essential, I couldn't say that.

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:24.060
I think that it's very, very short, and I don't even know how to say it without echoing exactly what Sean said.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:36.040
I mean, I think I just agree that it kind of will blur into the collection of other games that are doing a similar thing that get better over time.

00:39:36.040 --> 00:39:38.540
So I'm going to say not essential, but pretty cool.

